From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 19:29:22 -0400
Subject: fix: the review's remaining items: a Space's storage, workers and
 keys

The code review of 2026-10-15 (docs/history/review-2026-10-15.md) filed items
S-179 to S-189 in BACKLOG.md; the operator asked for them fixed too. One patch
at the tip, as patches 0065 and 0066: the fixes touch code of a dozen patches.

Sessions. A page's sessionStorage in a Space with cookies of its own comes back
after a restart and with a reopened tab: the session keeps the namespace of the
partition the tab's pages live in, the restore recreates it in the partition
the tab comes back to, and what is left over in each Space's jar a tab came
back to is deleted after the restore, as Chromium does for the default jar
(sessions S28). The crash bubble's Restore never puts a Blank Window into the
window it sits in, whose Spaces it cannot take; the next window's tabs join,
and that window shows the Space it showed (sessions S29). Two helpers in
content/public/browser/stedding_session_storage.h reach the controller's
namespace for a partition, which NavigationController does not offer, without
touching a header half of the tree includes; the notification ids and the
worker's window keep to .cc files and Stedding's own headers for the same
reason.

Workers. A site in a Space with cookies of its own may show notifications at
all: Chromium blocks the permission in any partition that is not the origin's
own, a rule for app and guest partitions, and the new test met it. The window
a Space's service worker opens is made in the worker's jar and filed in that
jar's Space; a persistent notification's id names a Space's jar, so a click on
it and its close are dispatched into that jar, not the default one its origin
maps to (sessions S30). A page in a Space with cookies of its own is offered
no payment app: a payment app's service worker, and the window it opens to
pay, live in the profile's jar, so Google Pay started in such a Space offered
the Google accounts signed in outside it (sessions S36, GitHub issue 25).

The rest. A restored tab's saved Space is read once, by the first window that
files it (spaces B2); a Space-pinned split is a row of the pinned run (pins
H14); the Arc import makes one Space of a Space named twice and bounds its
reads (import I3, I22, I23); the welcome flow keeps a behaviour clicked before
the profile's values answer (W12), opens a bookmarks file from its own window
(W3) and tints the first Space (W7); sleep and the archive keep a contenteditable
draft, a PDF and a page DevTools inspects, and a new sleep delay re-times a
running timer (tabs R6, archive A12); the archive page lists closed windows'
and splits' tabs and filters by a Space's id (archive A9); Stedding's WebUI
handlers drop a message of the wrong shape. On Windows, a Ctrl+Alt chord made
with the left Alt runs its command on a layout whose AltGr types a character on
that key (windows N14).

Why: each item is behaviour its spec states, data a user would lose, or a
message a page could crash the browser with.
Removable when: never as a whole; the next rebase folds each fix into the patch
that owns its code (patches/README.md, the round-patch exception).
